				<div id="essay">
					<h2>Staging a theatrical comeback</h2>
					<p>When Fiona&#8217;s older sister turned their family&#8217;s 100-year-old barn into a theater for kids, Fiona gave performances every summer for five years, from ages 3 through 7. At age 9, Fiona became involved in a theater project of her own&#8212;leading performances in the barn once again.</p>
					<p class="pullquote">Fiona spends much of her time dreaming about what she&#8212;and others&#8212;could be.</p>
					<p>She wrote a 20-minute play and enlisted the help of an older friend as co-director. They gathered a cast and crew of 12, rehearsed twice a week for six weeks, and put on two performances. In addition to directing, Fiona acted, designed and made costumes, chose music, and worked on choreography. All of this was done without any adult intervention. Fiona provided leadership, generated enthusiasm, and kept the kids engaged.</p>
					<p>The second summer, when Fiona was 10 years old, she planned and led a summer theater camp. For several weeks, nine children met once a week for theater and improvisational games. Much of the summer was spent rewriting the story of Little Red Riding Hood&#8212;Little Pink Pain in the Pants&#8212;that became the basis for their improvisations.</p>
					<p>Fiona&#8217;s commitment is impressive&#8212;last summer she gave up her much-loved swim lessons in order to free up time for the theater camp. Now she&#8217;s teaching herself to type in order to write a script for this summer&#8217;s project&#8212;The Adventures of King Midas," from one of her favorite books.</p>
					<p class="last">The barn allows Fiona to share her love of theater with her friends and to express herself in a way that is generous and positive. Realizing that she could aim too high, Fiona takes things one step at a time. She spends much of her time dreaming about what could be but always comes back to earth.</p>
				</div>
